===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|pas}}, {{m|enm|pase}}, {{m|enm|pace}}, from {{m|enm|passen||to pass}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# An [[opening]], road, or track, available for passing; especially, one through or over some dangerous or otherwise impracticable barrier such as a mountain range; a [[passageway]]; a defile; a ford.
#: {{synonyms|en|gap|notch}}
#: {{ux|en|[[mountain pass|mountain '''pass''']]}}
#* {{RQ:Longfellow Ballads|poem=Excelsior|stanza=4|page=130|passage="Try not the '''Pass'''!" the old man said; / "Dark lowers the tempest overhead, / The roaring torrent is deep and wide!" / And loud that clarion voice replied / Excelsior!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1910|first=Maud|last=Diver|authorlink=Maud Diver|chapter=The Valley of Decision|title=The Great Amulet
|url=https://archive.org/details/greatamulet00divegoog/|location=New York|publisher=w:John Lane Company|OCLC=7035847|page=368
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/greatamulet00divegoog/page/n380/|text=Followed two more weeks of marching,—rougher marching this time,—through the core of the lofty mountains that divide India from Central Asia; across the terrible Depsang Plains, seventeen thousand feet up; and over four '''passes''' choked with snow; till they came upon a deserted fort, set in the midst of stark space, and knew that here, indeed, was the limit of human habitation. Next day the work of exploration had begun in earnest.}}
# A [[channel]] connecting a river or body of water to the sea, for example at the mouth (delta) of a river.
#: {{ux|en|the '''passes''' of the Mississippi}}
# A single movement, especially of a hand, at, over{{,}} or along anything.
#* '''1921''', John Griffin, "Trailing the Grizzly in Oregon", in ''Forest and Stream'', pages 389-391 and 421-424, republished by Jeanette Prodgers in '''1997''' in ''The Only Good Bear is a Dead Bear'', page 35:
#*: [The bear] made a '''pass''' at the dog, but he swung out and above him {{...}}
# A single passage of a tool over something, or of something over a tool.
#: {{syn|en|transit}}
# An [[attempt]].
#: {{ux|en|My first '''pass''' at a career of writing proved unsuccessful.}}
## A [[sexual]] [[advance]] (often in the phrase {{m|en|make a pass}}).
##: {{synonyms|en|proposition|come-on}}
##: {{ux|en|The man kicked his friend out of the house after he made a '''pass''' at his wife.}}
# {{senseid|en|success in exam}} [[success|Success]] in an [[examination]] or similar [[test]].
#: {{ux|en|I gained three '''passes''' at A-level, in mathematics, French, and English literature.}}
# {{lb|en|fencing}} A thrust or push; an attempt to stab or strike an adversary.
#: {{syn|en|thrust}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A thrust; a sally of wit.
# {{lb|en|sports}} The act of moving the ball or puck from one player to another.
#: {{ux|en|Everyone in the football stadium expected a '''pass''' play on third down.}}
# {{lb|en|rail transport}} A passing of two trains in the same direction on a single track, when one is put into a [[siding]] to let the other overtake it.
#: {{ant|en|meet}}
# Permission or license to pass, or to go and come.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1826|author=w:James Kent|title=w:Commentaries on American Law
|passage=A ship sailing under the flag and '''pass''' of an enemy.}}
#: {{syn|en|access|admission|entry}}
# {{senseid|en|permitting document}} A document granting permission to pass or to go and come; a passport; a ticket permitting free transit or admission
#: {{ux|en|a railroad '''pass'''; a theater '''pass'''; a military '''pass'''}}
# {{lb|en|baseball}} An [[intentional walk]].
#: {{ux|en|Smith was given a '''pass''' after Jones' double.}}
# {{lb|en|sports}} The act of [[overtake|overtaking]]; an overtaking manoeuvre.
#* {{quote-journal|en|work=BBC Sport|date=13 September 2020|author=Andrew Benson|title=Tuscan Grand Prix: Lewis Hamilton claims 90th win after incredible race|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/formula1/54138847|passage=Albon made hard work of the result. Starting fourth, he dropped back to seventh at the second start and had to fight his way back up, which he did with some excellent '''passes'''.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260902201017/https://www.bbc.com/sport/formula1/54138847|archivedate=2 September 2026}}
# The state of things; condition; predicament; impasse.
#* {{RQ:Jack Straw|I|18|England is growne to ſuch a '''paſſe''' of late,<br>That rich men triumph to ſee the poore beg at their gate.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear|III|iv|passage=What, have his daughters brought him to this '''pass'''?}}
#* {{RQ:South Twelve Sermons|passage=Matters have been brought to this '''pass''', that, if one among a man's sons had any blemish, he laid him aside for the ministry...}}
#* {{RQ:Lindsay Redheap|page=187|passage="What with Robert strolling out with publicans' daughters, and you having affairs with bicycle-shop keepers, the family is coming to a pretty '''pass'''."}}
#: {{syn|en|condition|predicament|state}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Estimation; character.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare All's Well|II|v|passage=This '''passes''', Master Ford.}}
# {{lb|en|cookery}} The area in a [[restaurant]] [[kitchen]] where the finished dishes are passed from the chefs to the waiting staff.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2017|author=Fred Sirieix|title=Secret Service: Lifting the lid on the restaurant world|passage=The finished dishes are placed on the '''pass''' ready to be collected by the waiter.}}
# An act of declining to play one's turn in a game, often by saying the word "pass".
#: {{ux|en|A '''pass''' would have seen her win the game, but instead she gave a wrong answer and lost a point, putting her in second place.}}
# {{lb|en|computing}} A run through a document as part of a translation, compilation or reformatting process.
#: {{ux|en|Most Pascal compilers process source code in a single '''pass'''.}}

