{{also|Film}}
{{minitoc}}
==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:filme|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|filme}}, from {{inh|en|ang|filmen|t=film, membrane, thin skin, foreskin}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*filmīn-}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*filmīn-|t=thin skin, membrane}} (compare {{cog|gem-pro|*felma-|t=skin, hide}}), from {{der|en|ine-pro||*pél-mo-|t=membrane}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*pel-|t=to cover, skin}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|filmene|t=thin skin, human skin}}, {{cog|dum|velm}}, {{m|dum|vilm|t=fleece, film, membrane}}, {{cog|goh|felm|t=peel, skin, wrap}}, {{cog|ang|*felma}} (in {{m|ang|ǣġerfelma|t=egg membrane}}). Related also to {{cog|nl|vel|t=sheet, skin}}, {{cog|de|Fell|t=skin, hide, fur}}, {{cog|sv|fjäll|t=fur blanket, cloth, scale}}, {{cog|no|fille|t=rag, cloth}}, {{cog|lt|plėvē|t=membrane, scab}}, {{cog|ru|плева́|t=membrane}}, {{cog|grc|πέλμα|t=sole of the foot}}. More at [[fell#Etymology_2|fell]]. Sense of ''a thin coat of something'' is 1577, extended by 1845 to the coating of chemical gel on photographic plates. By 1895 this also meant the coating plus the paper or celluloid.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/fɪlm/|[fɪɫm]}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-film.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|[ˈfɪlm̩]|[ˈfɪləm]|a=Scotland,North East England,Ireland,Northern Ontario}}
* {{IPA|en|/fɪu̯m/|a=MLE,l-vocalization}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfɪl(ɪ)m/|a=Indic}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪlm|s=1}}
* {{hyph|en|film}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}
[[File:Kodak Ektar 100 135 film cartridge (01).jpg|thumb|a roll of '''film''']]

# A [[thin]] [[layer]] of some substance; a [[pellicle]]; a [[membranous]] covering, causing [[opacity]].
#: {{ux|en|a clear plastic '''film''' for wrapping food}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Works 1751|volume=I|title=Messiah|lines=39–40|page=40|passage=He from thick '''films''' ſhall purge the viſual ray, / And on the ſightleſs eye-ball pour the day: {{...}}}}
# {{senseid|en|Q6293}}{{lb|en|photography}} A [[medium]] used to [[capture]] [[images]] in a [[camera]].
# {{senseid|en|Q11424}}{{lb|en|countable}} The sequence of still images itself, which produces a moving image when played; a [[movie]].
# {{senseid|en|Q16144339}}{{lb|en|uncountable}} A visual art form that consists of a sequence of still images preserved on a recording medium to give the illusion of motion; [[movie]]s generally.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=3 March 2014|doi=10.3390/rel5010219|author=Zoe Alderton|title=‘Snapewives’ and ‘Snapeism’: A Fiction-Based Religion within the Harry Potter Fandom|work=Religions|publisher=MDPI|url=https://www.mdpi.com/2077-1444/5/1/219/htm|pages=219–257|volume=5|issue=1|passage=Despite personal schisms and differences in spiritual experience, there is a very coherent theology of Snape shared between the wives. To examine this manifestation of religious fandom, I will first discuss the canon scepticism and anti-Rowling sentiment that helps to contextualise the wider belief in Snape as a character who extends beyond book and '''film'''.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230925041649/https://www.mdpi.com/2077-1444/5/1/219/htm|archivedate=25 September 2023}}
# A [[professionally]] made [[movie]] of a [[substantial]] [[narrative]] work, around 1 to 3 hours long; a [[feature film]]; a [[cinematic]] [[movie]].
# A slender [[thread]], such as that of a [[cobweb]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Romeo and Juliet|act=I|scene=iv|passage=Her whip of cricket's bone, the lash of '''film'''.}}

