===Etymology 1===
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:sik|id=ill|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|sik}}, {{m|enm|sike}}, {{m|enm|seek}}, {{m|enm|seke}}, {{m|enm|seok}}, from {{inh|en|ang|sēoc|t=sick, ill}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*seuk}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*seukaz}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*sewg-|t=to be troubled or grieved}}.

See also {{cog|fy|siik}}, {{cog|nl|ziek}}, {{cog|de|siech}}, {{cog|nb|syk}}, {{cog|nn,sv|sjuk}}, {{cog|da|syg}}; also {{cog|mga|socht|t=silence, depression}}, {{cog|xcl|հիւծանիմ|t=to be weakening}}.

The "very good, excellent" sense is an {{ss|en|type=a}} from the original sense of "in poor health". Compare similar semantic development in {{l|en|terrific}} and {{l|en|wicked}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# <!--{{lb|en|less common in the|_|UK|and|Ireland|nocat=1}}-->In poor [[health]]; [[ill]].
#: {{syn|en|ill|not [[well]]|poorly|sickly|unwell|Thesaurus:diseased}}
#: {{ant|en|fit|healthy|well}}
#: {{ux|en|We have to care for the '''sick'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=a1420|year_published=1894|author=The British Museum Additional MS, 12,056
|by=w:Lanfranc of Milan|title=Lanfranc’s “Science of cirurgie.”
|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=6XktAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA63
|chapter=Wounds complicated by the Dislocation of a Bone|isbn=1163911380
|publisher=K. Paul, Trench, Trübner & Co|location=London|editor=Robert von Fleischhacker
|page=63|passage=Ne take noon hede to brynge togidere þe parties of þe boon þat is to-broken or dislocate, til viij. daies ben goon in þe wyntir, & v. in þe somer; for þanne it schal make quytture, and be '''sikir''' from swellynge; & þanne brynge togidere þe brynkis eiþer þe disiuncture after þe techynge þat schal be seid in þe chapitle of algebra.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=7|passage=‘Children crawled over each other like little grey worms in the gutters,’ he said. ‘The only red things about them were their buttocks and they were raw. Their faces looked as if snails had slimed on them and their mothers were like great '''sick''' beasts whose byres had never been cleared.{{nb...}}’}}
## {{+obj|en|:with}} Afflicted by (a specific condition, usually medical).
##: {{ux|en|She was '''sick''' all day with the flu.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''sick''' with a cold&mdash;'''sick''' with contagious diseases&mdash;'''sick''' with sciatica&mdash;'''sick''' with love&mdash;'''sick''' with worry}}
# Having an [[urge]] to [[vomit]].
#: {{syn|en|nauseated|Thesaurus:nauseated}}
#: {{ux|en|My daughter was violently '''sick''' three times in the night.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|title=The Texas criminal reports|page=8
|passage=In the meantime the old man had gotten up and gone out in the yard and  began to vomit. Henry said I believe I feel '''sick''' and got up and went out. He went out one door and his father went out the other one. I did not think there was anything wrong with the coffee and I asked my wife to pour this out {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1918|author=Cecil Day Lewis|title=The Whispering Roots|publisher=Jonathan Cape|page=140
|passage=Q. Didn&#39;t he complain he was '''sick''' before he commenced to vomit?<br>A. He did, just before he said, to me, “I feel '''sick''',” I asked him if he wanted to throw up and he said yes.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1958|author=Gene D’Olive|title=Chiara|publisher=Signet Book
|passage={{...}} trying hard to cry. Crying’s good. Crying teaches him to breathe. But I wish he weren’t crying from hunger. I feel dizzy. I sit down and feel a little '''sick'''. Maybe I’ll vomit, too. No, to never vomit. I feel '''sick''', but I won’t vomit. I never vomit.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Cheryl Rainfield|title=Stained|publisher=Houghton Mifflin Harcourt|isbn=9780547942100|page=38
|passage=I feel '''sick''', like I might vomit, and I'm more tired than I can ever remember feeling.}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} Mentally [[unstable]], [[disturbed]].
#: {{syn|en|disturbed|twisted|warped}}
#: {{ux|en|You '''sick''' bastard!}}
#* {{quote-song|en|author=Boy Pablo|year=2018|title=Sick Feeling|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hTFDlGv2IWg|text=What a '''sick''', '''sick''' feeling / To let you go, my dear}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} [[in bad taste|In bad taste]].
#: {{ux|en|That’s a '''sick''' joke.}}
