===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[spend]] the autumn (in a particular place).
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Northern Germany. A Sketch.|journal=[[w:Fraser's Magazine|Fraser’s Magazine for Town and Country]]|volume=XI|issue=LXV|month=May|year=1835|page=507|passage=True it is that, owing to the migratory propensities of our countrymen, every third man has wintered at Naples, springed at Vienna, summered in Switzerland, and '''autumned''' on the banks of the Lago Maggiore;}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=William C[yrus] Sprague|title=Tad, the Story of a Boy who Had No Chance|year=1912|page=2|passage=If Tad’s father and Tad had wintered, springed, summered, and '''autumned''' together for an hundred years instead of fifteen they could{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Mortimer Jones|title=Lines of No Importance|journal=The Alphi Phi Quarterly|year=1937|page=29|passage=They wintered in a warm place / And summered in a cold, / But where they springed and '''autumned''' / I never have been told.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:Princeton Alumni Weekly|volume=XXXIX|issue=8|date=18 November 1938|page=180|passage=''Floyd Waggaman'', the namesake of “Floyd’s Folly,” summered and '''autumned''' at Siasconset, which your columnist has been told is hard by Nantucket, Martha’s Vineyard, or one of the disenchanted isles.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=O. Henry Memorial Award Prize Stories|year=1944|page=111|passage=The major’s office, young pudgy Major Lewis whose wife once offered Joe a bag of peanuts and who summered in New England and early-'''autumned''' in New York.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Chambers’s Journal|year=1950|page=269|passage=She springed in London, summered in Stockholm, '''autumned''' at Vichy, and wintered at Monte Carlo.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Alistair Cooke|title=The Americans: Letters from America on Our Life and Times, 1969-79|year=1980|page=264|isbn=0-14-005691-2|passage=I have summered and '''autumned''' (fallen?) on the North Fork for forty-two years.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Tim Pratt|chapter=The Third-Quarter King|editors=w:Jonathan Strahan; Jeremy G Byrne|title=w:Eidolon I|year=2006|page=2|isbn=9780809562343|passage=In recent years his friend the fourth-quarter king summered, '''autumned''', and springed in nearby Southern California, which was how they stayed so easily in touch.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Larry Stettner; Bill Morrison|title=Cooking for the Common Good: The Birth of a Natural Foods Soup Kitchen|location=Berkeley, Calif.|publisher=w:North Atlantic Books|year=2010|page=84|isbn=978-1-55643-957-5|passage=Larry and Bill had planned to hold a white-linen “fancy” fund-raiser dinner in late June or early July, which would bring out the moneyed crowd who “summered” on the Island. If you summer or winter somewhere you are affluent, Larry knew. (Funny, though, he had never heard of anyone who “'''autumned'''” in Vermont or who was “springing” in Colorado.)}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Devin Gordon|title=So Many Ways to Lose: The Amazin’ True Story of the New York Mets—the Best Worst Team in Sports|publisher=[[w:Harper (publisher)|Harper]]|year=2021|isbn=9780062940049|passage=So when she summered with her family in the Mediterranean, she sailed across the ocean from New York, and when she '''autumned''' in Maine, her chauffeur drove her ten hours north through the foliage, and when winter thawed and it was time again for spring training, she met her Mets in Florida after a long journey down the coast by train.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To (cause to) undergo the changes associated with autumn, such as leaves changing color and falling from trees.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Horace Eaton Walker|title=The Lady of Dardale and Other Poems|location=Manchester, N.H.|publisher=Browne & Rowe|year=1886|pages=139 and 201|passage=The glistening path where weeds had clung, / And tumbled bushes lay, / Was hidden now, but yet there rung / Tones of an '''autumned''' May.{{...}}And cheers rang out, the song and shout, / For the fray had found its eve, / And pirate chief like '''autumned''' leaf, / O’er fallen pride did grieve!”}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Virginia Sandars|title=The Duke of Melton|journal=w:London Society|volume=LII|location=London|publisher=F. V. White & Co.|year=1887|page=185|passage={{...}}he himself roamed with innocent Kate through the fast '''autumning''' woods{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Antjie Krog|title=A Change of Tongue|year=2003|page=98|isbn=0-9584468-4-9|passage=She turns off towards the river, where the lawns lie '''autumned''' on the banks between the frayed golden willow branches.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jeffrey Winterborne|title=Medical Marijuana / Cannabis Cultivation: Trees of Life at the University of London|publisher=Pukka Press|year=2008|pages=249–250|isbn=978-0-9550112-2-1|passage={{...}}three quarters, two thirds of the flowering tops have changed colour and these are very resinous, solid buds and the colouration of the leaf, you can see how it’s yellowed off, it’s '''autumning''', autumn has come if you would like to put it that way.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Joyce Rupp|title=Anchors for the Soul: Daily Wisdom for Inspiration and Guidance|location=Notre Dame, Ind.|publisher=Sorin Books|year=2018|pages=8 and 9|isbn=978-1-932057-13-3|passage=Spirit of Autumn, When I grow tired of using my gifts to benefit others, take me to the '''autumned''' fields where earth freely yields the bounty of her summer.{{...}}All night a steady rain fell upon the '''autumned''' earth, moistening every dried crack of the bony summer, rinsing what lay tattered and soiled in the remnants of yesterday.}}

