{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|tool}}{{lb|en|shoemaking}} A [[tool#Noun|tool]] in the [[form#Noun|form]] of a [[foot#Noun|foot]] on which an [[item#Noun|item]] of [[footwear]] (such as a [[boot#Noun|boot]] or [[shoe#Noun|shoe]]) is [[place#Verb|placed]] for [[shape#Verb|shaping]] while it is being [[manufacture#Verb|manufactured]] or [[repair#Verb|repaired]].
#: {{hyponyms|en|bootlast|shoe-last}}
#: {{coordinate terms|en|boot tree|shoe tree}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Romeo and Juliet Q1-2|act=I|scene=ii|sig=&#91;B4&#93;|page=14|passage=I muſt to the learned to learne of them, that's as much to ſay, as the Taylor muſt meddle vvith his '''Laſte''', the Shoomaker vvith his needle, the Painter vvith his nets, and the Fiſher vvith his Penſill, I muſt to the learned.}}
#* {{RQ:Middleton Michaelmas Terme|sig=&#91;A4&#93;|page=12|passage=Here's gallants of all ſizes, of all '''laſts''', / Here you may fit your foote, make choyſe of thoſe / VVhome your affection may reioyce in.|footer=A {{lg|figurative}} use.}}
#* {{RQ:Heywood If You Know Not Me|sig=C2|verso=1|page=17|passage=Shoomaker, you goe alittle beyond your '''laſt'''.|footer=A variant of the phrase {{m|en|cobbler, keep to your last}}.}}
#* {{RQ:Purchas Pilgrimage|edition=2nd|chapter=Of the Religions and Other Rites of the Persians|page=372|passage=And leſſe matters ſet on the Friers '''laſts''', make ſeely Papiſts beleeue novv, that Proteſtants haue no Churches nor Religion, nor ſcarſely the ſhape of men.|footer=A figurative use.}}
#* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|title=The Noble Gentleman|act=III|scene=[ii]<!--OED-->|page=36|column=1|passage=Is he not Duke indeed, ſee hovv he lookes / As if his ſpirit vvere a '''laſt''', or tvvo / Above his veines, and ſtretcht his noble hide.|footer=A figurative use.}}
#* {{RQ:Dekker Middleton Honest Whore|part=2|sig=F2|page=48|passage=I ſet my braines vpon an vpright '''Laſt'''; tho my vvits be old, yet they are like a vvitherd pippin, vvholſome.|footer=A figurative use.}}
#* {{RQ:John Gay Trivia|page=3|passage=Let firm, vvell-hammer'd Soles protect thy Feet / Thro' freezing Snovvs, and Rains, and ſoaking Sleet. / Should the big '''Laſte''' extend the Shoe too vvide, / Each Stone vvill vvrench th' unvvary Step aſide: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens American Notes|volume=I|chapter=Philadelphia, and Its Solitary Prison|page=241|passage=Occasionally, there is a drowsy sound from some lone weaver's shuttle, or shoemaker's '''last''', but it is stifled by the thick walls and heavy dungeon-door, and only serves to make the general stillness more profound.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2006|month=September|author=Cathy Newman|title=Every Shoe Tells a Story|editor=[[w:Chris Johns (photographer)|Chris Johns]]|journal=w:National Geographic|archiveurl=|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=w:National Geographic Society|volume=210|issue=3|page=83|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/nationalgeograph210nati/page/n482/mode/1up|issn=0027-9358|oclc=1049714034|passage=How is an in-your-face black leather thigh-high lace-up boot with a four-inch spike heel like a man's black calf lace-up oxford? They are both made on a '''last''', the wood or plastic foot-shaped form that leather is stretched over and shaped to make a shoe.}}

