====Proper noun====
{{en-proper noun|~|s}}

# {{senseid|en|Q119}}The fifth [[month]] of the [[Gregorian calendar]], following [[April]] and preceding [[June]].
#: {{alti|en|mul:5<id:Q119>}}
#: {{syn|en|Fifth Month|q=Quakerism}}
#: {{hol|en|calendar year<id:Q3186692>|;|year<id:Q577>}}
#: {{comeronyms|en|January<id:Q108>|February<id:Q109>|March<id:Q110>|April<id:Q118>|June<id:Q120>|July<id:Q121>|August<id:Q122>|September<id:Q123>|October<id:Q124>|November<id:Q125>|December<id:Q126>}}
#* '''Before 1789''', "{{w|Barbara Allen (song)|Bonny Barbara Allen}}," traditional ballad, collected in Francis James Child and George Lyman Kittridge (1886), [https://archive.org/details/englishscottishp22chilrich/page/276/mode/2up ''The English and Scottish Popular Ballads'', vol. II, part II, p. 277]:
#*: All in the merry month of '''May''', / When green leaves they was springing, / This young man on his death-bed lay, / For the love of Barbara Allen.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Frank Jacobs|title=Welcome to Stanistan|magazine=w:Foreign Policy|url=https://foreignpolicy.com/2014/07/01/welcome-to-stanistan/|date=2014-07-01|passage=At a signing ceremony in the Kazakh capital, Astana, on '''May''' 29, the presidents of Russia, Belarus, and Kazakhstan ratified the Eurasian Economic Union (EEU) into existence. An EEU modeled on the European Union was first mooted back in 1994 by Kazakh President Nursultan Nazarbayev, but took off only after his Russian counterpart, Vladimir Putin, seized upon its potential as a Moscow-centered, Asia-oriented alternative to the EU.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251021010603/https://foreignpolicy.com/2014/07/01/welcome-to-stanistan/|archivedate=21 October 2025}}
#* {{RQ:Independent|author=Rachel Dobkin|title=Significant parts of the Constitution were quietly removed from the Congress website|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/constitution-removed-congress-website-restored-b2803241.html|date=2025-08-06|passage=In '''May''', White House deputy chief of staff for policy Stephen Miller told reporters the Trump administration is “looking at” suspending the writ of habeas corpus for migrants under claims of an “invasion.”|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250807051609/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/constitution-removed-congress-website-restored-b2803241.html|archivedate=7 August 2025}}
# {{given name|en|female}}, usually pet name for [[Mary]] and [[Margaret]], reinforced by the month and plant meaning.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1856|author=w:E. D. E. N. Southworth|title=The Widow's Son|publisher=T. B. Peterson|year_published=1867|page=210
|passage={{...}} I will not send Owen's Lily '''May''' to the almshouse." "Lily―''what''?" demanded Mrs. Morley rather sharply, for she was half provoked with what she mentally called Amy's whim of keeping the outcast child when she might send it to the asylum. "Lily '''May'''," said Amy, smiling. "Her name is Mary, and we called her first Little Mary, and then Little '''May'''. But Owen calls her Lily '''May'''."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1982|author=w:Ruth Rendell|title=The Fever Tree and Other Stories|publisher=Hutchinson|isbn=0091497302|page=119
|passage=Their parents named them June and '''May''' because their birthdays occurred in those months. {{...}} '''May''' was like the time of year in which she had been born, changeable, chilly and warm by turns, sullen yet able to know and show loveliness that couldn't last.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=w:Margaret Forster|title=Isa & May|publisher=Chatto & Windus|isbn=9780701184667|page=5
|passage=It's an awkward name: Isamay, pronounced Is-a-may. Isa is my paternal grandmother's name (shortened from Isabel) and '''May''' my maternal grandmother's (it comes, somehow, from Margaret). The amalgamation is, as you see, strictly alphabetical. Life, I feel, would have been much easier if they had chosen Maybel.}}
#* {{quote-journal |en |author=Hilary Bird |title=Baby named Sahaiʔa prompts changes to Vital Statistics Act |magazine=w:CBC News |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/north/nwt-aboriginal-font-official-id-1.3630353 |date=2016-06-13 |passage=Sahaiʔa '''May''' Talbot was born on Feb. 15, 2014. However, on her birth certificate, her name is spelled Sahai'a because the Northwest Territories government only allows the Roman alphabet to be used on official documents. |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20160613150454/http://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/north/nwt-aboriginal-font-official-id-1.3630353 |archivedate=2016-06-13}}
# {{surname|en|from=Middle English}}.
