# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[habituate]], [[accustom]], or [[inure]] (someone or something) to a particular use, purpose, or circumstance.
#: {{ux|en|to '''season''' oneself to a climate}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|by extension}} To [[prepare]] by [[dry]]ing or [[harden]]ing, or [[removal]] of natural [[juice]]s.
#: {{ux|en|The timber needs to be '''seasoned'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become [[mature]]; to grow fit for use; to become adapted to a climate.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become [[dry]] and [[hard]], by the escape of the natural juices, or by being penetrated with other substance.
#: {{ux|en|The wood has '''seasoned''' in the sun.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[mingle]]: to moderate, temper, or qualify by [[admixture]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[impregnate]] (literally or figuratively).
#* {{quote-book|en|1589|w:Richard Hakluyt|''The principall navigations, voiages and discoveries of the English nation''|https://archive.org/details/cihm_35668/page/n116/mode/1up|93|When the male hath once '''ſeaſoned''' the female, he neuer after toucheth her.|norm=When the male hath once '''seasoned''' the female, he never after toucheth her.|volume_plain=part 1|chapter=22|location=w:London}}
#* {{quote-book|en|1601|w:Philemon Holland|''The Historie of the World''||224|For this prince{{…}}would not ſuffer the Buls to come unto the Kine and '''ſeaſon''' them, before they were both foure yeares old.|volume_plain=book 8|url=https://books.google.ro/books?redir_esc=y&hl=ro&id=HiQ6KlZSt8MC&q=Pyrrhus#v=snippet&q=%22before%20they%20were%20both%22|section=chapter 45|location=w:London|original=w:[[Natural History (Pliny)|''Naturalis Historia'']]|by=w:Pliny the Elder}}
#* {{quote-book|en|1745||''A Collection of Voyages and Travels''||150|If you had '''seasoned''' me with that philosophy, which formeth the mind to ratiocination, and insensibly accustoms it to be satisfied with nothing but solid reasons, if you had given me those excellent precepts and doctrines, which raise the foul above the assaults of fortune, and reduce her to an unshakeable and always equal temper, and permit her not to be lifted up b prosperity, nor debased by adversity, if you had taken care to give me the knowledge of what we are, and what are the first principles of things, and had assisted me in forming in my mind a fit idea of the greatness of the universe, and of the admirable order and motion of the parts thereof, if, I say, you had instilled into me this kind of philosophy, I should think myself incomparably more obliged to you than ''Alexander'' was to his ''Aristotle''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|1763|Edmund Burton|''Antient Characters deduced from Classical Remains''||82|In minds, not '''seasoned''' and impregnated with the due apprehension of those ends, that conduce to ease and security, there is usually a tempestuous discontent, that raises unruly ferments; an unkind gale, by whose resistless powers, the port is overreached.}}

