===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# {{lb|en|literal}} Having a top that is far up (''contrast: ''[[deep]] ('''having a bottom that is far down' '').)
## {{lb|en|of a person or two-footed creature}} Having great [[vertical]] extent (usually reckoned from the bottom of the feet to the very top of the head) when standing. (For instance, somebody with a height of over 6 feet would generally be deemed tall.) 
##: {{ant|en|short}}
##: {{ux|en|Being '''tall''' is an advantage in basketball.}}
##: {{ux|en|[[ostrich|Ostrich]]es are the tallest living birds.}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=5|passage=Then came a maid with hand-bag and shawls, and after her a '''tall''' young lady. She stood for a moment holding her skirt above the grimy steps,{{...}}, and the light of the reflector fell full upon her.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|author={{w|Robert Bingham (writer)|Robert Bingham}}|title=Lightning on the Sun|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=7GZPnauKdk4C&q=%22journo%22%7C%22journos%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&dq=%22journo%22%7C%22journos%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=bDKRT-3XKaP3mAWW0rmBAg&redir_esc=y|page=34
|passage=He′d been packed in with all the other journos, standing out only in that he was '''taller''' than most and didn′t make as much noise.}}
## {{lb|en|of an inanimate thing, such as a building, hill, lamp, etc.}} Having its top a long way up; having a great [[vertical]] (and often greater than [[horizontal]]) extent.
##: {{syn|en|high|q=less common, old-styled}}
##: {{ant|en|low|low-rise|short}}
##: {{ux|en|'''Tall''' trees, at least about 30m high.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2018|author=w:Tim Flannery|title=Europe: A Natural History|page=55|passage=To him it seemed that the Bornean rainforests, the '''tallest''' on Earth, were places that had: 'remained untouched and unchanged since remote geological epochs[.]'}}
## {{senseid|en|coffee}}{{lb|en|specialized|chiefly|US|of a cup of coffee}} Smaller than [[grande]], usually 8 ounces (~ 230 ml).
##: {{cot|en|grande|venti}}
# {{lb|en|of a story}} Hard to believe, such as a [[tall story]] or a [[tall tale]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1870|journal=The Cornhill Magazine|volume=21|page=9
|passage="That's '''tall''' talk."<br>"Not an inch taller than the truth."}}
# ''Old senses that arose before the height-related senses''
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[obsequious|Obsequious]]; [[obedient]].
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[seemly|Seemly]]; [[suitable]]; [[fitting]], [[becoming]], [[comely]]; [[attractive]], [[handsome]].
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[bold|Bold]]; [[brave]]; [[courageous]]; [[valiant]].
## {{lb|en|archaic}} [[fine|Fine]]; [[proper]]; [[admirable]]; [[great]]; [[excellent]].

