{{also|Center}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|centre||Ireland|all Commonwealth countries including|_|UK|Canada|Australia|New Zealand|South Africa|etc.}}

===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:center<ety:bor<frm:centre<ety:der<la:centrum>>>>|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱent-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|center}}, {{m|enm|centre}}, from {{der|en|frm|centre}}, from {{der|en|la|centrum}}, from {{der|en|grc|κέντρον|}}, from {{m|grc|κεντεῖν||to prick, goad}}. {{doublet|en|centrum}}.
* {{sense|group theory}} In the notation ''Z''(''G''), the letter ''Z'' derives from the {{semantic loan|en|de|Zentrum||center|notext=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|en|non-rhotic}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈsɛn.təː/|[ˈsɛn.tʰəː]|a=RP}}
* {{a|en|rhotic}}
** {{a|en|GA}}
*** {{IPA|en|/ˈsɛn.tɚ/|[ˈsɛ̟n.tʰɚ]|~|[ˈsɛ̟n.tʰɹ̩]|a=non-nt-flapping}}
**** {{audio|en|en-us-center.ogg|a=US}}
*** {{IPA|en|[ˈsɛ̟ɾ̃.ɚ]|~|[ˈsɛ̟ɾ̃.ɹ̩]|a=nt-flapping}}
**** {{audio|en|en-US center (nasal flapping).ogg|a=US}}
** {{a|en|Southern US|AAVE|pen-pin}}
*** {{IPA|en|/ˈsɪn.tɚ/|[ˈsɪ̟n.tʰɚ]|~|[ˈsɪ̟n.tʰɹ̩]|a=non-nt-flapping}}
*** {{IPA|en|[ˈsɪ̟ɾ̃.ɚ]|~|[ˈsɪ̟ɾ̃.ɹ̩]|a=nt-flapping}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛntə(ɹ)|s=2}}
* {{hyph|en|cen|ter}}
* {{homophones|en|sinner|aa=pin-pen,nt-flapping}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}} {{tlb|en|American spelling|alternative|_|Canadian spelling}}

# The point in the interior of a [[circle]] that is [[equidistant]] from all points on the [[circumference]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1908|translator=w:Thomas L. Heath|author=Euclid|journal=Elements|section=III.9
|passage=If a point be taken within a circle, and more than two equal straight lines fall from the point on the circle, the point taken is the '''centre''' of the circle.}}
# The point in the interior of a [[sphere]] that is [[equidistant]] from all points on the [[circumference]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=David Adam|journal=The Guardian|date=4 Jun 2005
|passage=Japanese scientists are to explore the '''centre''' of the Earth. Using a giant drill ship launched next month, the researchers aim to be the first to punch a hole through the rocky crust that covers our planet and to reach the mantle below.}}
# The [[middle]] portion of something; the part well away from the [[edge]]s.
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} The [[innermost]] point of the [[Earth]], or the Earth itself, as the center or [[foundation]] of the [[Universe]]; the center or foundation of the Universe abstractly.
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=I|page=12|lines=73-75|passage=[...] their portion set <br>As far remov'd from God and light of Heav'n <br>As from the '''Center''' thrice to th'utmost Pole.|year=1873}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Winter's Tale|II|i|282|2|''Leo''. No: if I mistake <br>In those Foundations which I build upon, <br>The '''Centre''' is not bigge enough to beare <br>A Schoole-Boyes Top.}}
# {{lb|en|geometry}} The point on a [[line]] that is [[midway]] between the [[end]]s.
# {{lb|en|geometry}} The point in the interior of any [[figure]] of any number of [[dimension]]s that has as its [[coordinates]] the [[arithmetic mean]] of the coordinates of all points on the [[perimeter]] of the figure (or of all points in the [[interior]] for a center of volume).
#: {{ux|en|The [[centroid]] is one of the '''centres''' of a [[triangle]]}}
# {{lb|en|geometry}} A point on a triangle's plane that is in some sense in its middle.
#: {{ux|en|There are several '''centers''' of a triangle such as its [[circumcenter]], [[incenter]], [[orthocenter]] and centroid.}}
# A place, especially a [[building]] or [[complex]], set aside for some specified [[function]] or [[activity]].
#: {{coi|en|[[shopping center|shopping '''center''']], [[convention center|convention '''center''']]'', ''[[civic center|civic '''center''']]'', ''garment '''center''''', ''Lincoln '''Center''' for the Performing Arts'', ''Rockefeller '''Center'''}}
# {{lb|en|politics}} The [[ensemble]] of [[moderate]] or [[centrist]] [[political]] [[party|parties]].
# The [[venue]] in which the [[head of government]] in a {{w|Centralized government|centralized state}} is [[situated]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Pál Fodor|doi=10.1515/9783112209431|title=The Business of State. Ottoman Finance Administration and Ruling Elites in Transition (1580s–1615)|publisher=Klaus Schwarz Verlag × De Gruyter|location=Berlin|year=2018|year_published=2020|isbn=978-3-87997-477-1|series=Studien zur Sprache, Geschichte und Kultur der Turkvölker|seriesvolume=28|page=50|passage=In Anatolia tensions between state officials (''ehl-i örf'') and the peasants were strained to breaking point.<br/>At several places—particularly in the frontier provinces—there were fierce clashes between the janissaries stationed there and the governors. In Aleppo and Damascus incidents were common after 1589: the ''kul''s threw rocks at the ''beylerbeyi''’s house, killed people, broke into the ''divan'' several times and took the money prepared by the council for remittance to the '''centre'''.}}
# A [[topic]] that is particularly [[important]] in a given context, the [[element]] in a [[subject]] of [[cognition]], [[volition]] or [[discussion]] that is [[perceive]]d as [[decisive]].
#: {{ux|en|the '''center''' of the controversy}}
#: {{ux|en|the '''center''' of attention}}
# {{lb|en|sports}} A player in the middle of a playing area.
## {{lb|en|basketball}} The player, generally the tallest, who plays closest to the basket.
## {{lb|en|ice hockey}} The [[forward]] that generally plays between the [[left wing]] and [[right wing]] and usually takes the [[faceoff]]s.
## {{lb|en|American football|Canadian football}} The person who holds the ball at the beginning of each play.
## {{lb|en|netball}} A player who can go all over the court, except the [[shooting circle]]s.
## {{lb|en|soccer}} A [[pass]] played into the centre of the pitch.
##* {{quote-web
|en
|date=December 28, 2010
|author=Owen Phillips
|title=Sunderland 0 - 2 Blackpool
|work=BBC
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/9317216.stm
|page=
|passage=Bent twice sent efforts wide of the far post after cutting in from the left, Wellbeck missed his kick from an inviting '''centre''' and failed to get on the end of a looping pass when six yards out.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230724164201/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/9317216.stm|archivedate=24 July 2023}}
## {{lb|en|rugby}} One of the [[back]]s operating in a central area of the pitch, either the [[inside centre]] or [[outside centre]].
##* {{quote-web
|en
|date=February 4, 2011
|author=Gareth Roberts
|title=Wales 19-26 England
|work=BBC
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/rugby_union/9386391.stm
|page=
|passage=Gatland's side got back to within striking distance when fly-half Jones's clever pass sent '''centre''' Jonathan Davies arcing round Shontayne Hape.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260910100522/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/rugby_union/9386391.stm|archivedate=10 September 2026}}
# A certain [[mechanical]] [[implement]] [[function]]ing in the [[middle]] of a [[whole]] [[apparatus]]
## {{senseid|en|temporary support}}{{lb|en|architecture}} A [[temporary]] [[structure]] upon which the materials of a [[vault]] or [[arch]] are supported in position until the work becomes self-supporting.
## {{lb|en|engineering}} One of the two [[conical]] [[steel]] [[pin]]s in a [[lathe]], etc., upon which the work is held, and about which it [[revolve]]s.
## {{lb|en|engineering}} A conical [[recess]] or [[indentation]] in the end of a [[shaft]] or other work, to receive the point of a center, on which the work can turn, as in a lathe.
## {{lb|en|AU|NZ}} The ring in the gambling game [[two-up]] in which the [[spinner]] operates.
# {{lb|en|algebra|group theory|ring theory}} The set of those [[elements]] (of a given [[algebraic structure]]) that [[commute]] with every other element, usually denoted ''Z(G)''. In the case of ([[semigroup|semi-]])[[group]]s, it is required that they commute under the (semi-)group operation; in the case of rings and ([[Lie algebra|Lie]]) algebras, under multiplication (i.e. the [[bracket]] for Lie algebras)

====Synonyms====
