===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q11570}} In the [[International System of Units]], the [[base unit]] of [[mass]]; conceived of as the mass of one [[litre]] of water, but now defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the Planck constant {{math|''h''}} to be 6.626&nbsp;070&nbsp;15&nbsp;×&nbsp;10<sup>-34</sup> when expressed in units of kg⋅m<sup>2</sup>⋅s<sup>−1</sup>. Symbol: [[kg]]
#: {{hol|en|megagram<id:Q11776930>|<|gigagram<id:Q2799294>}}
#: {{mer|en|picogram<id:Q3239557>|<|nanogram<id:Q2282906>|<|microgram<id:Q1645498>|<|milligram<id:Q3241121>|<|gram<id:Q41803>}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Matthew Robinson and Lianne Kolirin|title=The world has just redefined the kilogram|url=https://www.cnn.com/2018/11/13/world/kilo-measurement-scli-intl|date=2018-11-13|passage=Friday’s vote has permanently redefined the '''kilogram''' and sent Le Grand K into retirement.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260223063139/https://www.cnn.com/2018/11/13/world/kilo-measurement-scli-intl|archivedate=23 February 2026}}
# {{lb|en|proscribed}} The unit of [[weight]] such that a one-kilogram mass is also a one-kilogram weight.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1990|journal=Central Asia|issue=26-27|page=137
|passage=A nan (bread) costs 25 Afghanis : a '''kilogram''' of beef 800 Afghanis and mutton of the same weight for 1200 Afghanis; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2015-07-22|author=Reshma M. Khan; et al.|title=Embolic Stroke as the Initial Manifestation of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus|journal=Case Reports in Rheumatology|url=http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4525759/|doi=10.1155/2015/373201|volume=2015|issue=|pages=
|passage=Review of systems was positive for unintentional weight gain of 13.5 '''kilograms''' in the last eight months, excessive fatigue, fever, difficulty focusing, one episode of painful oral ulcer on the roof of her mouth, change in the hair texture with nonscarring alopecia, shortness of breath with exertion, and constipation.}}

====Usage notes====
* {{sense|proscribed|unit of weight}} The use of the kilogram as a unit of weight is somewhat imprecise, as weight can change while mass remains constant. The weight of a one-kilogram mass will depend on its location because the pull of gravity varies from one place to another. It is therefore frequently proscribed but is nonetheless in wide use (e.g., a person's weight in kilograms). (The same imprecision and proscription also occur with many other words pertaining to weight and mass, such as the verb {{m|en|weigh}}.)
* Whilst one kilogram equals 1,000 grams, it is the kilogram and not the gram that is the base unit.

