short.content:
{{also|Short|shórt}}
==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(s)ker-|id=cut}}
{{ety|en|id=having a small distance|:inh|enm:schort<id:short>|tree=1}}

From {{inh|en|enm|schort}}, {{m|enm|short}}, from {{inh|en|ang|sċeort}}, {{m|ang|sċort|t=short}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*skurt}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*skurtaz|t=short}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*(s)ker-}}. {{doublet|en|shirt|skirt|curt}}.

{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|sco|short}}, {{m|sco|schort|t=short}}, {{cog|fr|court}}, {{cog|nl|kort}}, {{cog|de|kurz}}, {{cog|goh|scurz|t=short}} (whence {{cog|gmh|schurz}}), {{cog|non|skorta|t=to lack}} (whence {{cog|da|skorte}}), {{cog|sq|shkurt|t=short, brief}}, {{cog|la|curtus|t=shortened, incomplete}} and {{cog|sla-pro|*kortъkъ}}. See more at {{m|en|shirt}}.
{{col-bottom}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|en|horse-hoarse}}
** {{IPA|en|/ʃɔːt/|a=RP}}
** {{IPA|en|/ʃoːt/|a=SSB,AU,NZ}}
*** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Back ache-short.wav|a=SSB}}
*** {{audio|en|En-au-short.ogg|a=AU}}
** {{IPA|en|/ʃoɹt/|[ʃo̞ɹt̚]|a=GA}}, {{enPR|shôrt}}
*** {{audio|en|En-us-short.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{a|en|non-horse-hoarse}}
** {{IPA|en|/ʃɔɾt/|a=Scotland}}
** {{IPA|en|/ʃɔːt/|a=Manchester}}
** {{IPA|en|/ʃɒt/|a=Eastern New England}}
** {{IPA|en|/ʃoɹt/|a=early 20th century <<GA>>,near-obsolete}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔː(ɹ)t|s=1}}
* {{hyph|en|short}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# Having a small distance from one end or edge to another, either [[horizontal]]ly or [[vertical]]ly.
#: {{syn|en|low|narrow|slim|shallow}}
#: {{ant|en|long|tall|high|broad|deep}}
# Of a person, living being{{,}} or object, having a comparatively small [[height]].
#: {{syn|en|little|pint-sized|petite|titchy<q:slang>}}
#: {{ant|en|tall}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=HarryBlank|work=w:SCP Foundation|title=Mind Over Matter|date=2022-09-22|url=https://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/8-mind-over-matter|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240523031638/https://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/8-mind-over-matter|archivedate=23 May 2024|accessdate=24 May 2024|text=Nhung Ngo had the '''shortest''' legs at Site-43. She was the '''shortest''' member of staff, two inches beneath the positively elfin Delfina Ibanez, and yet Lillian found her inexplicably difficult to shake. Power-walking down the halls didn't do the trick, as it always did when Wettle-dodging, since the diminutive headshrink kept disappearing into commissaries or service corridors or even other people's offices and emerging, smiling, in front of her.}}
# Having little [[duration]].
#: {{syn|en|brief|concise}}
#: {{ant|en|long}}
#: {{ux|en|Our meeting was a '''short''' six minutes today. Every day for the past month it’s been at least twenty minutes long.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1980|author=Alvin Toffler|title=The Third Wave|page=230
|passage=The results of this generalized speedup of the corporate metabolism are multiple: '''shorter''' product life cycles, more leasing and renting, more frequent buying and selling, more ephemeral consumption patterns, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2012|month=March-April|author=Anna Lena Phillips|title=Sneaky Silk Moths|volume=100|issue=2|page=172|magazine=w:American Scientist|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/sneaky-silk-moths|passage=Last spring, the periodical cicadas emerged across eastern North America. Their vast numbers and '''short''' above-ground life spans inspired awe and irritation in humans—and made for good meals for birds and small mammals.|accessdate=24 May 2013|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130219220644/https://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/sneaky-silk-moths|archivedate=19 February 2013}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Kristen Rogers|title=People with ADHD have shorter life expectancy and higher risk of mental health issues, study finds|url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/01/23/health/adhd-shorter-life-expectancy-study-wellness/index.html|date=2025-01-23|passage=Having attention deficit hyperactivity disorder, or ADHD, is associated with a '''shorter''' life expectancy and a greater risk of mental health issues, according to a new study of more than 30,000 people with the disorder in the United Kingdom.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260605210949/https://www.cnn.com/2025/01/23/health/adhd-shorter-life-expectancy-study-wellness/index.html|archivedate=5 June 2026}}
# {{lb|en|followed by '''''for'''''}} Of a word or phrase, constituting an abbreviation (for another) or shortened form (of another).
#: {{ux|en|"Phone" is '''short''' for "telephone" and "asap" '''short''' for "as soon as possible".}}
# {{lb|en|cricket}} Of a [[fielder]] or fielding [[position]], that is relatively [[close]] to the [[batsman]].
#: {{ant|en|long}}
# {{lb|en|cricket}} Of a ball, bowled so that it bounces relatively far from the batsman.
# {{lb|en|golf}} Of an [[approach shot]] or [[putt]], that falls short of the green or the hole.
# {{lb|en|gambling}} Of betting [[odds]], offering a small return for the money wagered.
# {{lb|en|baking}} Of [[pastry|pastries]] or {{lb|en|metallurgy}} of materials, [[brittle]], [[crumbly]].
#: {{ux|en|[['''short'''bread]]}}
#: {{ux|en|[[hot '''short''']]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Heston Blumenthal|title=Historic Heston|isbn=1620402343|page=122|passage=I chose to interpret the references to butter and sugar as indicating that a '''short''' pastry was required. (Later editions suggest a biscuit-like texture.)}}
# [[abrupt|Abrupt]], [[brief]], [[pointed]], [[curt]].
#: {{ux|en|He gave a '''short''' answer to the question.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Edwin Drood|passage=“We are '''short''' to-night!” cries the woman, with a propitiatory laugh. “Short and snappish we are! {{...}}”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Charles E. Schwarz|title=Murder at the Table of Fools|passage=The reason I was a tad '''short''' with Start, Leeper and Burnsome was my fear they, with their education, experience and degrees, would see what I had denied for all these years{{nb...}}.|}}
# Limited in quantity; inadequate; insufficient; scanty.
#: {{ux|en|a '''short''' supply of provisions}}
# Insufficiently provided; inadequately supplied, especially with money; scantily furnished; lacking.
#: {{ux|en|to be '''short''' of money}}
#: {{ux|en|I'd lend you the cash but I'm a little '''short''' at present.}}
#: {{ux|en|The cashier came up '''short''' ten dollars on his morning shift.}}
# Deficient; less; not coming up to a measure or standard.
#: {{ux|en|an account which is '''short''' of the truth}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1829|author=w:Walter Savage Landor|title=Imaginary Conversations|passage={{...}}the people are worn down with taxes, and hardly anything '''short''' of an invasion could rouse them again to war.|volume=IV|chapter=The Emperor Alexander and Capo D'Istria}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} [[undiluted|Undiluted]]; [[neat]].
#* {{RQ:Dickens Dombey and Son|passage=“There ain’t no drain of nothing '''short''' handy, is there?” said the Chicken, generally. “This here sluicing night is hard lines to a man as lives on his condition.”<br>Captain Cuttle proffered a glass of rum {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Linda Chaikin|title=Desert Rose
|passage=Delance raised his beer and watched Hoadly throw down another swig of hard stuff. "Take it '''short''' if you want to make it over the mountain tonight."}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Not distant in time; near at hand.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=IV|canto=XII|stanza=18|passage=Marinell was sore offended / That his departure thence should be so '''short'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Clarendon History|passage=He commanded those who were appointed to attend him to be ready by a '''short''' day.}}
#* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|volume=I
|passage=But, alas! he who escapes from death is not pardoned; he is only reprieved, and reprieved to a '''short''' day.}}
# {{sid|en|finance}} {{lb|en|finance}} Being in a financial investment position that is structured to be profitable if the price of the underlying security declines in the future.
#: {{ant|en|long}}
#: {{cot|en|long<id:finance>}}
#: {{coi|en|[[short position|'''short''' position]]}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm '''short''' in General Motors because I think their sales are plunging.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Doubtful of, skeptical of.
# {{lb|en|finance|dated}} Of [[money]], given in the fewest possible notes, i.e. those of the largest [[denomination]].
#: {{ant|en|long}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1909|author=James Blyth|title=The member for Easterby|page=296
|passage=He pulled a cheque-book from his pocket, and drew for two hundred thousand pounds. “I'll take it '''short''',” he said {{...}}}}

====Usage notes====
* {{m|en||Short}} is often used in the positive [[vertical]] dimension and used as is {{m|en|shallow}} in the negative vertical dimension; in the horizontal dimension, {{m|en|narrow}} is more commonly used.

