====Usage notes====
* Within most English dialects, ''American'' now refers particularly to the people of the most populous English-speaking country in the Americas, the United States of America. Other senses now typically require qualifiers: {{m|en|Central American}}, {{m|en|Native American}}, etc. Some speakers maintain separate idiolects, however, including {{w|Native American name controversy|some Native Americans}}. Many Latin Americans also {{w|American (word)|pointedly maintain}} Brazilian and European Portuguese's preference for using {{m|pt|Américas}} to refer to the Americas generally, and {{m|pt|estado-unidense//estadunidense}} ({{m|es|estadounidense}} in Spanish) when referring to both the country and its inhabitants.
* Usage of unqualified ''American'' as a language separate from or in place of {{m|en|English}}, though once sincere, is now typically considered a sign of poor education or used to mock others' supposed ignorance, parochialism, or excessive nationalism.

